## 3. Specify and estimate competing models

We will fit an intercept-only model and three increasingly complex substantive models.

| Model | Formula | Research rationale |
|:-----------------------|:-----------------------|:-----------------------|
| M0: Null | `stayed ~ 1` | Baseline model |
| M1: Education | `stayed ~ satisfaction` | Staying together varies with relationship satisfaction |
| M2: Education + Income | `stayed ~ satisfaction + avoidance` | Avoidant attachment explains additional variation |
| M3: Expanded | `stayed ~ satisfactoin + avoidance + cohabiting` | Cohabiting explains additional variation |

The models are **nested**: each successive model contains all predictors from the preceding model, plus one or more additional predictors.

```{r}
#| label: fit-models
mod0 <- glmmTMB(stayed ~ 1,data = couples,
  family = binomial(link = "logit"))

mod1 <- glmmTMB(
  stayed ~ satisfaction,
  data = couples,
  family = binomial(link = "logit"))

mod2 <- glmmTMB(
  stayed ~ satisfaction + avoidance + cohabiting,
  data = couples,
  family = binomial(link = "logit"))

mod3 <- glmmTMB(
  stayed ~ satisfaction * cohabiting + avoidance,
  data = couples,
  family = binomial(link = "logit"))

```

After executing the model-fitting code, explore the results in the **R Console**:

``` r
summary(mod0)
summary(mod1)
summary(mod2)
summary(mod3)

confint(mod3)
```

