## 8. Interpret the results

### Overall model performance

### Coefficients are on the log-odds scale

```{r}
#| label: mod2-fixef
fixef(mod2)
```

For a 1-point increase in satisfaction, holding the satisfaction constant, the **log-odds** of staying together change by `r round(fixef(mod2)$cond[["satisfaction"]], 2)`.

### Transform log-odds into odds ratios

```{r}
#| label: or-exp
exp(fixef(mod2)$cond)
```

```{r}
#| label: or-ci
or_ci <- exp(confint(mod2, parm = "beta_"))
or_ci
```

Holding avoidance constant, a 1-point higher satisfaction score is associated with about `r round(exp(fixef(mod2)$cond[["satisfaction"]]), 2)`-times the odds of still being together. Or is `r round(exp(fixef(mod2)$cond[["satisfaction"]]), 2)` more likely.

### Interpreting satisfaction

Holding avoidance constant, a one-unit increase in relationship satisfaction is associated with a **`r round(coefficient_results |> filter(term == "satisfaction") |> pull(estimate), 2)`-fold** change in the odds of a couple dissolving, 95% CI \[`[r round(coefficient_results |> filter(term == "satisfaction") |> pull(conf.low), 2), r round(coefficient_results |> filter(term == "satisfaction") |> pull(conf.high), 2)`\].

### Interpreting avoidance

Holding relationship satisfaction constant, a one-unit increase in avoidance is associated with a **`r round(coefficient_results |> filter(term == "avoidance") |> pull(estimate), 2)`-fold** change in the odds of a couple dissolving, 95% CI \[`r round(coefficient_results |> filter(term == "avoidance") |> pull(conf.low), 2)`, `r round(coefficient_results |> filter(term == "avoidance") |> pull(conf.high), 2)`\].

### Interpreting cohabiting category (if it were present in the model)

If the selected model included `cohabiting`, its coefficients represent the odds of staying together relative to the reference cohabiting category, holding constant satisfaction and avoidance. These are adjusted group differences, not differences between the raw category means.

### Substantive conclusions

This analysis illustrates how relationship dissolution can be investigated using several competing binary logistic regression models.

The model-comparison process allows us to evaluate whether additional predictors explain meaningful variation beyond simpler models.

The final model provides estimates of the odds of a couple staying together or dissolving.
